//
// A view BOUND INSIDE a branch arm whose container is reassigned IN THE SAME ARM is copied at
// the bind, exactly as it is when the two statements sit at the top level (loft#1394).
//
// `(B-Disturb)` names reassigning the container as one of the three events that end the place
// a view names, and `(B-View)` materialises a view live across one.  The walk that names those
// views reads statements in source order — but it handled a `Set` whose right-hand side is a
// value branch WHOLE, through the `leaf` arm its own doc calls "deliberately coarse in both
// directions".  So a bind inside an arm and a reassignment inside the SAME arm were never
// separated in time, and nothing materialised:
//
//     got = match sh { Holder{inner} => { sh = Empty{z: 0}; inner.a }, _ => -1 }
//         --interpret 0 (the new subject's bytes at the payload's offset)   --native 1
//     got = if c { x = h.inner; h = Hold{…}; x.a } else { -1 }
//         BOTH backends 5 — the NEW container's value, where the top-level twin reads 1
//
// The coarseness is right for a form whose internal order is unknown; a `Set`'s is not — the
// value runs, then the target is written.  `ViewWalk` now walks such a statement in that
// order, and the target's own establishment is read at the point the slot is written.
//
// Two facts had to travel with the deps for the copy to actually happen.  A materialised view
// stops being a view, so the never-free mark it was given as one is lifted (`clear_skip_free`)
// — a `match`/`is` payload binding is marked by the parser, and stripping only the deps left
// it owning a store nothing released.  And a binding that carries NO deps is admitted beside
// one that names the container: the `is` spelling of a payload capture has none where its
// `match` twin does, and there is nothing to strip but still a mark to lift.
//
// ⚠ Nor is the view that IS an arm's VALUE (`x = if k > 0 { h.inner } else { … }`), which
// still reads its container after the reassignment — 5 where the twin above reads 1, on both
// backends for a struct tail and split for a collection one.  Both halves of the NAMING can be
// made to see it and were measured doing so; the emitters have no per-arm copy, and stripping
// the deps without one makes the binding an owner of a store it only views, whose scope-exit
// free then names the container's store.  Measured, backed out, filed as loft#1396 — freezing
// today's answer in a cell here would be freezing a wrong one.
//
// ⚠ The `w.st = Empty{…}` shape is NOT here, and its absence is the point.  `(B-Disturb)` says
// in as many words that OVERWRITING a place is not disturbing it — "`o.inner = Box{…}` writes
// INTO the place `o.inner` already occupies, so a view of it survives" — so a view of that
// place reads what is there now, on both backends, and 0 is the answer the rules give.  What
// that shape exposes is a different question (a payload binding outliving its variant), filed
// apart.
//
// @falsified-at: 6c09de23 — interpret exit 1 -> 0, interpret 4 assertion failures -> 0, native
//   exit 1 -> 0, native 1 assertion failure -> 0.
//   ⚠ That control is on no public ref, so this receipt records a falsification that
//   cannot be re-run — see TESTING.md § The patch receipt for the form that can be.
//   HOLDS: leak, panic, free-refusal, expectations are equal on both trees. Falsify prints
//   only the channels that MOVE and requires HERE to be clean, so each of those is clean on
//   both. The native side was not scored in this sweep.

struct Pay { a: integer, b: text }
enum Subj { Holder { inner: Pay }, Empty { z: integer } }
struct Hold { inner: Pay, t: text }

fn mk(n: integer) -> Pay { Pay{a: n, b: "n{n}"} }

fn grow(v: vector<Pay>) -> vector<Pay> {
  r: vector<Pay> = [];
  for e in v { r += [mk(e.a + 10)]; }
  r
}

// THE DEFECT — a payload binding and its subject's reassignment in one `match` arm.
fn test_a_match_arm_reassigning_its_own_subject() {
  sh: Subj = Holder{inner: mk(1)};
  got = match sh { Holder{inner} => { sh = Empty{z: 0}; inner.a }, _ => -1 };
  assert(got == 1, "the payload the arm was entered with: got {got}, want 1");
}

// The `is` spelling of the same capture — the one whose binding carries no deps at all.
fn test_an_is_capture_reassigning_its_own_subject() {
  sh: Subj = Holder{inner: mk(1)};
  got = -1;
  if sh is Holder { inner } {
    sh = Empty{z: 0};
    got = inner.a;
  }
  assert(got == 1, "the `is` capture: got {got}, want 1");
}

// Reassigned to the SAME variant with a different payload — the tag does not change, and the
// place still ends.
fn test_a_same_variant_reassignment_inside_the_arm() {
  sh: Subj = Holder{inner: mk(1)};
  got = match sh { Holder{inner} => { sh = Holder{inner: mk(7)}; inner.a }, _ => -1 };
  assert(got == 1, "same variant, new payload: got {got}, want 1");
}

// A PLAIN struct view bound inside an `if` arm — not an enum payload, and wrong on BOTH
// backends before the fix, which is what says the hole was the ordering and not the tag.
fn test_a_struct_view_bound_inside_an_if_arm() {
  h = Hold{inner: mk(1), t: "h"};
  c = true;
  got = if c { x = h.inner; h = Hold{inner: mk(5), t: "k"}; x.a } else { -1 };
  assert(got == 1, "struct view in an arm: got {got}, want 1");
}

// A vector-ELEMENT view in the same position, which was already right — the control that says
// the fix did not buy the struct cell by breaking this one.
fn test_a_vector_element_view_inside_an_arm_is_unchanged() {
  v = [mk(1)];
  c = true;
  got = if c { x = v[0] ?? mk(0); v = grow(v); x.a } else { -1 };
  assert(got == 1, "element view in an arm: got {got}, want 1");
}

// CONTROL — the bind inside the arm, the reassignment AFTER the whole statement.  Nothing is
// disturbed while the view is live, so it reads what it always did.
fn test_a_reassignment_after_the_statement_is_not_a_disturbance() {
  sh: Subj = Holder{inner: mk(1)};
  got = match sh { Holder{inner} => inner.a, _ => -1 };
  sh = Empty{z: 0};
  assert(got == 1, "read inside the arm, reassigned after: got {got}, want 1");
}

// CONTROL — an UNDISTURBED view bound inside an arm still ALIASES.  A fix that copied every
// view bound in a branch would pass every cell above and fail this one.
fn test_an_undisturbed_view_bound_in_an_arm_still_aliases() {
  sh: Subj = Holder{inner: mk(1)};
  c = true;
  got = if c { x = sh.inner; x.a = 9; x.a } else { -1 };
  assert(got == 9, "the view reads its own write: got {got}, want 9");
  assert(sh.inner.a == 9, "and it reaches the subject: got {sh.inner.a}, want 9");
}

// CONTROL — an arm that reassigns nothing is untouched.
fn test_an_arm_that_disturbs_nothing_is_untouched() {
  sh: Subj = Holder{inner: mk(4)};
  got = match sh { Holder{inner} => inner.a, _ => -1 };
  assert(got == 4, "plain arm: got {got}, want 4");
}

// CONTROL — a LOOP runs the arm again, so the second pass reads what the first pass left.
// The materialise must not freeze the first iteration's value.
fn test_a_loop_reads_each_passs_own_subject() {
  sh: Subj = Holder{inner: mk(1)};
  got = -1;
  for _ in 0..2 { got = match sh { Holder{inner} => { sh = Holder{inner: mk(7)}; inner.a }, _ => -1 }; }
  assert(got == 7, "the second pass reads the first pass's value: got {got}, want 7");
}
